There's a problem called "priority inversion", which is when the current thread
that hold the lock has _lower priority_ and then the scheduler decided to switch
to another thread that has _higher priority_ that also need the lock, without
releasing the lock on previous thread.

Example for "priority inversion":
Let's say we have thread 1 (T1) and thread 2 (T2). At first we run T2 until it's
doing I/O, which make the CPU scheduler switch to T1. When T1 run, T1 grab the
lock and enter the critical section. And then, before T1 exit critical section,
T2 finish I/O and because T2 has higher priority than T1, the CPU scheduler
switch to T2 and pause the operation on T1. When T2 trying to enter the same
critical section as T1, T2 can't get the lock so T2 waiting for the lock to be
available. The problem is that, the lock is in T1 and the CPU scheduler won't
switch to T1 because T2, which has higher priority, have not finish _yet_. So
the system is hung because the thread is keep waiting for the lock.

To solve the "priority inversion" problem, there are 2 ways (according to the
book, read "aside: more reason to avoid spinning: priority inversion"):
- Use "priority inheritance", which means the higher priority thread that is
  waiting for the lower priority thread can temporarily boost the lower thread's
  priority and enabling lower thread to run and overcoming the inversion.
- Ensure all threads have the same priority.

